### Page 3 of 15

```text
Annexation – UDC-13-00071 – Swan Ranch II Annexation
Staff Report
July 2, 2013
Page 3

SUMMARY:
The owners of this 882-acre area west of and adjacent to Clear Creek Parkway on the west side
of Interstate 25, north of and adjacent to Tundra Drive, have petitioned the City to annex the land
into the City in order to develop an extension of the Swan Ranch Rail Park. Clear Creek Parkway
and Tundra Drive are not included in the annexation in order to maintain their eligibility for
certain state grants for funding for improvements. Those roads will likely be annexed with the
next annexation in Swan Ranch Rail Park, at which time road maintenance including snow
plowing would become the City’s responsibility.
Wyoming State Statutes require that prior to annexing territory to the City certain steps must be
followed, including the governing body conducting a public hearing and finding that the territory
is eligible for annexation. The public hearing was conducted April 22, 2013. The statutes list six
criteria that must be met for the territory to be eligible for annexation. The attached resolution
finds compliance with these six criteria. The attached ordinance would approve the annexation,
allowing the annexation map to be recorded and the property brought into the City.
The current County zoning is Light Industrial (LI) and Planned Unit Development (PUD). The
applicant has submitted a City zoning proposal that includes LI zoning for a portion of the
annexation area and a City PUD designed to accommodate the unique circumstances in the
development for the remainder of the annexation. The intent is that the PUD could be adopted
for other rail-served properties in future annexations in Swan Ranch as well.
The most immediate concern with the annexation is provision of adequate fire-fighting services.
As noted in the comments from Cheyenne Fire & Rescue toward the end of this report, the
approximate response time to the area proposed for annexation is eight to nine minutes –
substantially longer than the four-minute standard set for the City.
In order to address this concern a new fire station is required in the vicinity. There are several
components required for a fully functioning fire station:
1. Land,
2. Building,
3. Equipment (including fire engine), and
4. Staff
A preliminary approximation for the cost to build and equip a new fire station, including a new
fire truck but excluding purchase of land, approaches $7 million. Staffing a fire station also
requires approximately $1 million annually, a portion of which may be expected to be recouped
from taxes and fees on the development.
A new fire station would benefit other property in the general area beyond Swan Ranch as those
areas annex to the City and development occurs.
In staff’s opinion there are several possible courses of action:
1. Approve the annexation with no action toward establishing a new fire station in the
vicinity.
2. Approve the annexation conditioned on dedication of land for a new fire station and
begin to study the adoption of impact fees or other methods to fund its construction and
equipment.
3. Approve the annexation conditioned on dedication of land and construction of the fire
station by the developer, with the City pursuing equipping the station.
4. Deny the annexation.
```

### Page 4 of 15

```text
Annexation – UDC-13-00071 – Swan Ranch II Annexation
Staff Report
July 2, 2013
Page 4

In staff’s opinion, simply annexing the land without any provision of a fire station in the area
could expose the annexation to challenge in court since it would be difficult to defend against
claims that the annexation does not further protection of health, safety, and welfare. At the other
extreme, denying the annexation would likely just lead to its continued development in the
County, though it would impact the development in terms of fees and rates for water and sewer
and may have other negative impacts. Staff feels the annexation can and should be contingent
upon dedication of land for a fire station and possibly construction of the fire station by the
developer. Additionally, it is important that the Governing Body consider how to equip the
station and how to address similar situations with expanding need for sanitation and road
maintenance equipment, as well future transportation improvements.
Using general revenue funding to fund the fire station and other necessary services would take
that funding away from other projects and priorities and require all taxpayers in the City to pay
for improvements necessitated by an individual development. On the other hand, requiring this
developer to dedicate the land and pay the entire cost of construction benefits other landowners
in the area without compensation from those other landowners. Establishing impact fees for these
improvements would spread the cost among the landowners as their properties develop. For
further discussion of impact fees, please see the section of this report on the Comprehensive
Plan.

Facing south on Clear Creek Parkway – subject property on right
