obtained building permits in the County prior to the annexation being initiated. The lot south of
Longhaul contains a regional detention pond. Another lot in the annexation area is proposed for a
Cheyenne Light, Fuel & Power substation. An unplatted parcel north of the proposed Wallick
Road has also obtained a site plan for through the County.
A preliminary plat was approved and includes areas beyond the annexation to the north and west
as well as the east side of Clear Creek Parkway up to I-25.
Swan Ranch, LLC, and Clear Creak Land Company own over 7,000 acres on either side of I-25.
A 2011 development agreement is in place for over 4,000 acres of that area, which includes the
current annexation. Under this agreement the City reimbursed the developer for $300,000 toward
the cost of the installation of a sewer main line for economic development purposes and agreed
to purchase land for open space from the developer with the park fees to be obtained from the
development of the property. Therefore, park fees have already been paid for the entire Swan
Ranch area as long as it develops in the City.
Water lines in the Swan Ranch Rail Park, south of the current annexation, have been installed by
the developer and given to the Board of Public Utilities, which leases them back to the
developer. The water currently comes from well water stored in the water tower in Swan Ranch
but will be City-treated water by June of 2014. If the property is not annexed, outside user
agreements are required and system development fees and monthly rates are 50 percent higher
for City water and sewer service.
COMPREHENSIVE PLAN:
LAND-USE AND ZONING: The future land-use plan in PlanCheyenne indicates Mixed-Use
Commercial Emphasis, Mixed-Use Employment Emphasis, Mixed-Use Residential Emphasis,
and Commercial Business for this area. Laramie County has zoned the subject property LI, Light
Industrial, and PUD, Planned Unit Development, with industrial uses permitted in the PUD. A
concurrent zone change is in process to establish City zoning of LI and PUD for the property.
Although the area is not designated as industrial on the future land use plan of PlanCheyenne, the
area to the south has developed as an industrial park and that development is proposed to
continue north to include this property and adjacent land. The most logical City zoning for the
annexation, other than PUD, is Light Industrial and Heavy Industrial. PlanCheyenne is currently
being updated and will likely reflect the types of uses being proposed for development in Swan
Ranch.
POLICIES AND PRINCIPLES: The proposed development already has an approved preliminary
plat in place and meets several goals and policies contained in PlanCheyenne, particularly those
relating to employment and economic development. For example, Principle 3.1 states: “Our
community will continue to promote a thriving and vital economy.” The Swan Ranch Rail Park
has already added jobs to the local economy and further development is likely to do so as well.
Additionally, the development is within the Urban Service Boundary, which meets the first Key
Land Use Concept in PlanCheyenne.
The location of the rail park does, however, cause some conflict with Foundation 7 of
PlanCheyenne – Developing in a Fiscally Responsible Way. Foundation 7 states that
PlanCheyenne “aims to guide future growth to promote efficient use of public and private
resources and to provide adequate public facilities. On the private side, new development should
“pay its own way” and provide the necessary services for the new development concurrent with
the development.” The City’s current public facilities and staffing levels are not adequate to
serve the build-out of the proposed development without reductions in levels of service, as noted
```

### Page 7 of 15

```text
Annexation – UDC-13-00071 – Swan Ranch II Annexation
Staff Report
July 2, 2013
Page 7

in the comments below from Public Works, Police, and Fire. Since the City has not established
impact fees other than Park Fees, which are used to acquire and improve park and open space
land, Board of Public Utilities System Development Fees, which are used for water and sewer
infrastructure, new development does not cover the costs of maintaining adequate levels of
service. Infill development and redevelopment are less likely to overtax existing resources
because they take advantage of infrastructure that is already in place and do not require services
to be extended to new areas. In the past, growth in new areas has occurred slow enough that the
City has attempted to absorb the increasing demands on service as development builds out. The
scale of the development occurring in the Swan Ranch Rail Park and its distance from areas
already being served make it unlikely that the City will be able to continue making up for
increasing demands incrementally with grants and existing resources. PlanCheyenne contains a
priority code revision of establishing impact fees following background studies which are
necessary to “establish an acceptable level of service and reasonable fee based on the actual
demand associated with a development.” (See ShapeCheyenne Policy 7.2.b, BuildCheyenne
Strategy 7.b, and Appendix B, Section 9.)
Two ways of addressing concerns relating to a development’s effect on levels of service are an
adequate public facilities ordinance, which is evaluated at the time of platting, and impact fees
collected at the time of building permits. Adequate public facilities ordinances, in conjunction
with the capital facilities planning process, establish minimum levels of service and require that
if a particular development action would reduce levels of service below that threshold, the
developer must address the reduction through land dedication, making public improvements, or
contributing funds to pay for public improvements. The funds must be kept separate from other
funds and used only for that purpose and typically within a specified timeframe or they would
have to be returned to the developer. These improvements often will be benefit other landowners
beyond the development boundaries, however.
Impacts fees are a consistent way to ensure that all development contributes to offsetting the
impacts of the development, rather than a large development such as Swan Ranch being
burdened with the entire cost, although the City could adopt both impact fees and an adequate
facilities ordinance. Even with impact fees in place, it would take time for funds to accumulate to
levels necessary to address the impacts of the proposed development. Therefore, an adequate
public facilities ordinance may be necessary as well.
Impact fees can provide more predictability for developers since they know upfront what the
costs will be and the time spent negotiating can thus be streamlined. With impact fees, all
development other than those specifically exempted would pay into the fund based on the size
and type of development proposed.
Impact fees need to be based on an independent study to establish what would be a reasonable
charge based on the type and scale of development proposed. For example, Weld County,
Colorado, based their fees on a 53-page, October, 2010 report. (Weld County collects a road
impact fee of $2,269 per new single-family dwelling unit and $2,043 per 1,000 square feet of
new industrial construction, in addition to a County facility impact fee of $636 per new single-
family dwelling unit and $152 per 1,000 square feet of new industrial construction.) The cost of
such studies, which may be roughly estimated to be in the range of $50,000-$75,000, can be
folded into the fee once established. An interim impact fee ordinance while a study is being
conducted may be an option to consider as well.
In 2007, the MPO initiated a discussion of impact fees for Laramie County to address concerns
regarding roads. A report, Fiscal Impact Feasibility Analysis, was presented to the County with
```

