```text
Annexation – UDC-13-00071 – Swan Ranch II Annexation
Staff Report
July 2, 2013
Page 8

recommendations to pursue additional information. At the time, Laramie County Commissioners
did not elect to move forward.
The criteria for annexation may have been adopted with this type of problem in mind. However,
since this property is currently vacant land other than the one lot under construction the
annexation itself will not jeopardize the City’s health, safety, and welfare; rather it is the
subsequent development that raises concerns. If the City had provisions in place for addressing
these concerns with platting and development, the annexation could be said to have minimal
impact. Since Cheyenne does not have such provisions, it is difficult to ensure that the
annexation and development will not result in a reduction of levels of service or other problems
in the future.
Initial conversations with the developer indicate that there is mutual appreciation of the need for
a fire station and possible support for establishing impact fees. Staff recommends initiating the
process to evaluate adoption of an impact fee and/or adequate facilities ordinance and exploring
options for procuring the necessary study.
If the developer of the subject property is willing to dedicate the necessary land for a future fire
station that would address the first step toward eventually having a functioning fire station to
serve the area and the annexation criteria for protection of health, safety, and welfare. The City
could then pursue establishing impact fees, which would help fund the construction and
equipping of the station. As mentioned, a study, as well as public discussion, would be required
to determine the appropriate level of fees.
TRANSPORTATION: The Transportation Plan 2030 Roadway Vision Plan anticipates a new
arterial connection (designated “Overland Trails Road”) to Otto Road over a mile to the
northwest and a new arterial road between Clear Creek Parkway and Overland Trails Road. The
Buildout Roadway Vision Plan anticipates an additional interchange and crossing at I-25 in the
Swan Ranch area between High Plains and College, as well as another additional arterial
connection to Otto Road. The crossing at I-25 is likely to be the planned Wallick Road, not
shown on the annexation map.
Similar to the Land Use discussion, the vision for this area has changed significantly since the
adoption of PlanCheyenne in 2006.  The assumptions for the transportation network were based
on the land uses identified at that time, which included a mix of residential and commercial.  The
Transportation Plan is also being updated as a part of PlanCheyenne and will reflect the change
in Land Use along with corresponding changes impacting the Transportation Plan.
As mentioned previously, Tundra and Clear Creek are not included in the annexation; however,
those roads will likely need to be annexed in the future with subsequent annexations. At that
point, road maintenance and snow plowing will become the City’s responsibility, creating higher
demands for limited resources (equipment, fuel, personnel).
Clear Creek Parkway is currently a three-lane road (two lanes plus a middle left-turn lane),
though it may need to be widened to five in the future, depending on the amount of traffic
generated by development in Swan Ranch. A new development agreement that will address
potential future road improvements is under review and will be presented to City Council for
approval. The developer is encouraged to set aside a portion of the proceeds from the sale of land
in the development for future road widening.
